.TH MONSW 7 "31 October 2026" "monsw"
.SH NAME
monsw \- the monitor switch between the chipset display and a display card
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /dev/monsw
.br
.B #include <sys/monsw.h>
.SH DESCRIPTION
Some display cards share the monitor with the Amiga's own picture: one or
two lines of the CIA-B port (CTS and SEL of the serial and parallel ports)
switch a monitor or a KVM between the two.
.B monsw
owns those lines.
It is a loadable kernel module of the demand class: it loads at the first
open of
.BR /dev/monsw ,
or before a card driver that depends on it.
A card driver switches through its kernel interface; programs use the
device, as
.BR monswctl (1M)
does.
.SS The value
The value says which lines switch the monitor and at which level.
It is a sum of these bits, the meanings of the Z3660's own
.B monitor_switch
setting:
.TP 6
.B 1
drive CTS (CIA-B port A, bit 4);
.TP
.B 2
drive SEL (bit 2);
.TP
.B 16
CTS low is the card's picture (without it, CTS high is);
.TP
.B 32
SEL low is the card's picture.
.PP
0 touches no line.
The valid values are 0 to 3, 16 to 19, 32 to 35 and 48 to 51; another value
is reported once on the console and taken as 0.
For example, 34 switches with SEL, the card's picture when SEL is low.
.SS Where the value comes from
The first of these that is set is the value in effect:
.TP 4
1.
the module parameter
.BR config ,
0 or more, in
.BR /etc/conf/modreg.local.d/monsw :
the value is then forced, and the next source is refused;
.TP
2.
the value set with
.BR MONSWIOC_SETCFG ,
root only.
At every boot,
.B /etc/rc2.d/S20monsw
sets the
.B monsw
line of
.BR /etc/display ;
when that file has no such line, the first line of the older file
.BR /etc/monsw ,
and the boot log says so; when neither has a value it sets nothing;
.TP
3.
the value the first card driver offered when it registered, its seed
(the Z3660 offers its firmware setting);
.TP
4.
otherwise 0.
.PP
A value that cannot be set at boot is one line on the console and in the
boot log, and the boot goes on.
.SS Switching
Only the lines the value names are touched, and nothing is driven until
the first switch.
The switch returns to the chipset's picture at the last close of the device
when the program that asked for the card's picture holds it, so a program
that dies cannot leave the monitor on the card.
.SH IOCTLS
.TP
.B MONSWIOC_RTG
switch to the card's picture.
.TP
.B MONSWIOC_NATIVE
switch to the chipset's picture.
.TP
.B MONSWIOC_GETCFG
fill a
.BR "struct monsw_info" :
the value in effect, its source, the state, the holder flag and the number
of cards registered.
.TP
.B MONSWIOC_SETCFG
the argument is the value itself, or \-1 to unset it.
Root only
.RB ( EPERM ).
.B EINVAL
for a value that is not a valid sum,
.B EBUSY
while a value is forced by the parameter.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /dev/monsw
character major 52, minor 0.
.TP
.B /etc/conf/mod.d/monsw
the module.
.TP
.B /etc/init.d/monsw
the boot step, linked as
.BR /etc/rc2.d/S20monsw .
.TP
.BR /etc/display ", " /etc/monsw
the value set at boot.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR monswctl (1M),
.BR display (4),
.BR display.d (4),
.BR xstart (1)
